t-slate-300 leading-8\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"space-y-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"p\",\"0\",{\"children\":[\"â€¢ \",\"Explain the basic principle of computed tomography.\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"1\",{\"children\":[\"â€¢ \",\"Describe how X-rays are used to generate CT images.\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"2\",{\"children\":[\"â€¢ \",\"Understand the role of the X-ray tube, detectors, and gantry.\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"3\",{\"children\":[\"â€¢ \",\"Explain how attenuation information is converted into CT images.\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"4\",{\"children\":[\"â€¢ \",\"Understand the basic concept of Hounsfield Units.\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"5\",{\"children\":[\"â€¢ \",\"Recognize the main components of a CT scanner.\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"6\",{\"children\":[\"â€¢ \",\"Understand the difference between axial, coronal, and sagittal reconstructions.\"]}]]}]}]]}],[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"className\":\"text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6\",\"children\":\"Lesson Content\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"space-y-8\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"border-l-2 border-brand-cyan/30 pl-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3\",\"children\":\"What Is Computed Tomography?\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-slate-300 leading-8\",\"children\":\"Computed Tomography is an imaging technique that acquires multiple X-ray measurements from different angles around the patient. A computer processes these measurements to reconstruct cross-sectional images, allowing anatomical structures to be evaluated without the superimposition commonly seen on conventional radiographs.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"border-l-2 border-brand-cyan/30 pl-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3\",\"children\":\"Basic CT Imaging Principle\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-slate-300 leading-8\",\"children\":\"During a CT examination, the X-ray tube rotates around the patient while detectors measure the radiation that passes through the body. Different tissues attenuate the X-ray beam to different degrees. The collected attenuation data are reconstructed mathematically to create an image representing the internal anatomy.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"2\",{\"className\":\"border-l-2 border-brand-cyan/30 pl-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3\",\"children\":\"Main Components of a CT Scanner\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-slate-300 leading-8\",\"children\":\"A CT system consists of several major components, including the X-ray tube, detector array, gantry, patient table, data acquisition system, reconstruction computer, and operator console. Each component contributes to the acquisition, processing, reconstruction, or display of CT images.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"3\",{\"className\":\"border-l-2 border-brand-cyan/30 pl-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3\",\"children\":\"X-Ray Tube\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-slate-300 leading-8\",\"children\":\"The X-ray tube generates the radiation used for CT imaging. During scanning, the tube rotates around the patient and produces a controlled X-ray beam. Tube voltage and tube current are important technical factors that influence image quality and radiation dose.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"4\",{\"className\":\"border-l-2 border-brand-cyan/30 pl-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3\",\"children\":\"CT Detectors\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-slate-300 leading-8\",\"children\":\"CT detectors measure the intensity of X-rays after they pass through the patient. Modern detector systems contain multiple detector elements arranged in rows, allowing the scanner to acquire data from multiple sections of the patient during a single rotation.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"5\",{\"className\":\"border-l-2 border-brand-cyan/30 pl-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3\",\"children\":\"Gantry and Patient Table\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-slate-300 leading-8\",\"children\":\"The gantry contains the rotating X-ray tube and detector system. The patient table moves the patient through the gantry during scanning. Accurate table positioning and patient centering are important for maintaining image quality and achieving appropriate dose distribution.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"6\",{\"className\":\"border-l-2 border-brand-cyan/30 pl-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3\",\"children\":\"X-Ray Attenuation\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-slate-300 leading-8\",\"children\":\"X-ray attenuation describes the reduction in X-ray intensity as the beam passes through the body. Attenuation depends on factors such as tissue composition, density, thickness, and X-ray energy. CT reconstruction uses these measurements to determine the relative attenuation of tissues.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"7\",{\"className\":\"border-l-2 border-brand-cyan/30 pl-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3\",\"children\":\"Hounsfield Units\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-slate-300 leading-8\",\"children\":\"CT image values are commonly expressed using Hounsfield Units (HU), which provide a standardized scale for describing tissue attenuation. Water is assigned a value of approximately 0 HU, while air has a value near -1000 HU. Dense materials such as cortical bone have substantially higher positive values.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"8\",{\"className\":\"border-l-2 border-brand-cyan/30 pl-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3\",\"children\":\"CT Image Reconstruction\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-slate-300 leading-8\",\"children\":\"After data acquisition, reconstruction algorithms convert the measured attenuation information into cross-sectional images. Different reconstruction approaches can influence spatial resolution, image noise, and the visual appearance of anatomical structures.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"9\",{\"className\":\"border-l-2 border-brand-cyan/30 pl-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3\",\"children\":\"Multiplanar Reconstructions\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-slate-300 leading-8\",\"children\":\"CT data can be reconstructed in multiple anatomical planes. Axial images represent transverse sections, while coronal and sagittal reconstructions provide additional views of anatomy. Three-dimensional and other advanced reconstructions may also be generated when clinically appropriate.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"10\",{\"className\":\"border-l-2 border-brand-cyan/30 pl-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3\",\"children\":\"Patient Positioning and Centering\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-slate-300 leading-8\",\"children\":\"Correct patient positioning and centering within the gantry are essential for consistent image quality and radiation dose optimization. Incorrect positioning can affect automatic exposure control, image uniformity, and the accuracy of the examination.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"11\",{\"className\":\"border-l-2 border-brand-cyan/30 pl-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3\",\"children\":\"Dose and Image Quality\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-slate-300 leading-8\",\"children\":\"CT protocols must balance radiation dose and diagnostic image quality. Increasing technical factors may improve image quality in some circumstances but can also increase patient radiation exposure. Appropriate protocol selection and optimization are therefore fundamental aspects of CT practice.\"}]]}]]}]]}],\"$undefined\",[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"className\":\"text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6\",\"children\":\"Educational Images\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",\"CT_L01_01_CT_Scanner_Anatomy.jpg-0\",{\"className\":\"overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-brand-border bg-slate-900\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"img\",null,{\"src\":\"/assets/images/en/courses/ct-mastery/lesson-01/CT_L01_01_CT_Scanner_Anatomy.jpg\",\"alt\":\"CT Scanner Fundamentals - 1\",\"className\":\"w-full h-auto object-contain\",\"loading\":\"lazy\"}]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"CT_L01_02_CT_Image_Formation.jpg-1\",{\"className\":\"overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-brand-border bg-slate-900\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"img\",null,{\"src\":\"/assets/images/en/courses/ct-mastery/lesson-01/CT_L01_02_CT_Image_Formation.jpg\",\"alt\":\"CT Scanner Fundamentals - 2\",\"className\":\"w-full h-auto object-contain\",\"loading\":\"lazy\"}]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"CT_L01_03_Pixel_vs_Voxel.jpg-2\",{\"className\":\"overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-brand-border bg-slate-900\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"img\",null,{\"src\":\"/assets/images/en/courses/ct-mastery/lesson-01/CT_L01_03_Pixel_vs_Voxel.jpg\",\"alt\":\"CT Scanner Fundamentals - 3\",\"className\":\"w-full h-auto object-contain\",\"loading\":\"lazy\"}]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"CT_L01_04_CT_Axial_Coronal_Sagittal.jpg-3\",{\"className\":\"overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-brand-border bg-slate-900\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"img\",null,{\"src\":\"/assets/images/en/courses/ct-mastery/lesson-01/CT_L01_04_CT_Axial_Coronal_Sagittal.jpg\",\"alt\":\"CT Scanner Fundamentals - 4\",\"className\":\"w-full h-auto object-contain\",\"loading\":\"lazy\"}]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"CT_L01_05_Hounsfield_Units.jpg-4\",{\"className\":\"overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-brand-border bg-slate-900\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"img\",null,{\"src\":\"/assets/images/en/courses/ct-mastery/lesson-01/CT_L01_05_Hounsfield_Units.jpg\",\"alt\":\"CT Scanner Fundamentals - 5\",\"className\":\"w-full h-auto object-contain\",\"loading\":\"lazy\"}]}]]}]]}],\"$undefined\",[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"className\":\"text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4\",\"children\":\"Key Points\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"text-slate-300 leading-8\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"space-y-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"p\",\"0\",{\"children\":[\"â€¢ \",\"CT uses rotating X-ray measurements and computer reconstruction to create cross-sectional images.\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"1\",{\"children\":[\"â€¢ \",\"The X-ray tube and detector system are central components of CT image acquisition.\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"2\",{\"children\":[\"â€¢ \",\"CT images represent differences in X-ray attenuation within the body.\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"3\",{\"children\":[\"â€¢ \",\"Hounsfield Units provide a standardized method for describing CT attenuation values.\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"4\",{\"children\":[\"â€¢ \",\"Axial, coronal, and sagittal reconstructions allow anatomy to be assessed in different planes.\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"5\",{\"children\":[\"â€¢ \",\"Patient positioning and centering influence both image quality and radiation dose.\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"6\",{\"children\":[\"â€¢ \",\"CT protocols should maintain diagnostic quality while optimizing radiation exposure.\"]}]]}]}]]}],\"$undefined\",\"$undefined\"]}]]}]]}]}]\nb:[[\"$\",\"meta\",\"0\",{\"name\":\"viewport\",\"content\":\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"1\",{\"charSet\":\"utf-8\"}],[\"$\",\"title\",\"2\",{\"children\":\"CT Scanner Fundamentals | Hidden Radiology\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"3\",{\"name\":\"description\",\"content\":\"CT Scanner Fundamentals\"}],[\"$\",\"link\",\"4\",{\"rel\":\"shortcut icon\",\"href\":\"/logos/hr-icon.png\"}],[\"$\",\"link\",\"5\",{\"rel\":\"icon\",\"href\":\"/logos/hr-icon.png\"}],[\"$\",\"link\",\"6\",{\"rel\":\"apple-touch-icon\",\"href\":\"/logos/hr-icon.png\"}]]\n1:null\n",